Yes. You can execute this Hadoop MapReduce program from the Ubuntu terminal as follows.

 ## 1\. Save the Java program

 Open Terminal and create the file:

```
mkdir -p ~/wordcount
cd ~/wordcount

nano WordCount.java
```

 Paste your complete Java code into the file, then save:

 - `Ctrl + O`
- Enter
- `Ctrl + X`

 Your file should be named exactly:

```
WordCount.java
```

 ## 2\. Check Hadoop is running

 If you are using Hadoop in pseudo-distributed mode, start HDFS and YARN:

```
start-dfs.sh
start-yarn.sh
```

 Check the Java/Hadoop processes:

```
jps
```

 You should normally see processes such as:

```
NameNode
DataNode
ResourceManager
NodeManager
SecondaryNameNode
```

 You can also check:

```
hdfs dfs -ls /
```

 If this command works, HDFS is available.

 ## 3\. Create an input file

 Create a local text file:

```
nano input.txt
```

 For example, enter:

```
Hello Hadoop
Hello World
Hadoop is powerful
Word Count using Hadoop
```

 Save it with `Ctrl + O`, Enter, then `Ctrl + X`.

 Check it:

```
cat input.txt
```

 ## 4\. Put the input file into HDFS

 Create an HDFS input directory:

```
hdfs dfs -mkdir -p /user/$USER/wordcount/input
```

 Upload the file:

```
hdfs dfs -put input.txt /user/$USER/wordcount/input/
```

 Verify:

```
hdfs dfs -ls /user/$USER/wordcount/input
```

 You should see:

```
input.txt
```

 ## 5\. Compile the Java program

 First find your Hadoop installation:

```
which hadoop
```

 Then compile:

```
mkdir -p classes

javac -classpath "$(hadoop classpath)" -d classes WordCount.java
```

 If there are no errors, create the JAR:

```
jar -cvf wordcount.jar -C classes .
```

 Check:

```
ls
```

 You should have something like:

```
WordCount.java
input.txt
classes/
wordcount.jar
```

 ## 6\. Run the MapReduce program

 Run:

```
hadoop jar wordcount.jar WordCount /user/$USER/wordcount/input /user/$USER/wordcount/output
```

 The two arguments correspond to:

```
WordCount <input-directory> <output-directory>
```

 So your Java code:

```
FileInputFormat.addInputPath(job, new Path(args[0]));
FileOutputFormat.setOutputPath(job, new Path(args[1]));
```

 receives:

```
args[0] = /user/$USER/wordcount/input
args[1] = /user/$USER/wordcount/output
```

 ## 7\. View the output

 After the job finishes successfully:

```
hdfs dfs -ls /user/$USER/wordcount/output
```

 You will normally see:

```
_SUCCESS
part-r-00000
```

 Display the result:

```
hdfs dfs -cat /user/$USER/wordcount/output/part-r-00000
```

 For the sample input, the output will be approximately:

```
count    1
hadoop   3
hello    2
is       1
powerful 1
using    1
word     1
world    1
```

 The exact counts depend on your input.

 ## 8\. Important: output directory already exists

 If you run the program again with the same output directory, Hadoop will usually give an error such as:

```
Output directory ... already exists
```

 Delete the old output first:

```
hdfs dfs -rm -r /user/$USER/wordcount/output
```

 Then run again:

```
hadoop jar wordcount.jar WordCount /user/$USER/wordcount/input /user/$USER/wordcount/output
```

 ## Complete command sequence

 If Hadoop is already installed and configured, you can essentially do:

```
cd ~/wordcount

mkdir -p classes

javac -classpath "$(hadoop classpath)" -d classes WordCount.java

jar -cvf wordcount.jar -C classes .

hdfs dfs -mkdir -p /user/$USER/wordcount/input

hdfs dfs -put -f input.txt /user/$USER/wordcount/input/

hdfs dfs -rm -r -f /user/$USER/wordcount/output

hadoop jar wordcount.jar WordCount \
    /user/$USER/wordcount/input \
    /user/$USER/wordcount/output

hdfs dfs -cat /user/$USER/wordcount/output/part-r-00000
```

 ### One thing to watch for

 Your code uses:

```
word.set(itr.nextToken().toLowerCase().replaceAll("[^a-zA-Z]", ""));
```

 So punctuation is removed and words are converted to lowercase. For example:

```
Hadoop!
HADOOP
hadoop.
```

 will all be counted as:

```
hadoop
```

 If you get an error while compiling or running it, **paste the exact terminal error here** and I can tell you the specific command to fix it.